ows  how to handle employees that and to keep  them happy so how did Adobe go so wrong  with their customers  to really understand this dichotomy  between employees and customers we first  have to understand why customers hate  Adobe so much in the first place all of  this can be traced back to 2012 when  Adobe introduced Creative Cloud Creative  Cloud had completely uprooted adobe's  Revenue model instead of having  customers pay a large one-time licensing  fees of a 500 or 700 for a specific  version of a software they forced people  to jump onto an expensive subscription  planner which currently costs 55 per  month or 660 dollars per year for  professionals who already had licenses  to several Adobe apps and were  constantly upgrading this was actually a  great deal for example if you had  licenses to five Adobe apps that each  cost 700 and you upgraded them once  every five years you were spending at  thirty five hundred dollars every five  years and only getting access to five  apps at a fixed version with the  subscription on the other hand you would  not only be paying less at 3300 for real  five years but you would have access to  all of adobe's apps and always have the  latest version as well the problem  though was that the vast majority of  adobe's users didn't fall within this  category the average user only needed  one maybe two apps if we assume that  they upgraded it at the same frequency  of once every five years we're talking  about a fiber cost of 700 or 1400 and  that's only if they bought the whole  software again usually there were  upgrade options that cost 200 or 300  with the subscription model however they  now had to pay 3 300 for every five  years for little in return now you might  be thinking why not just get the  individual plants well the individual  plans are rip-offs and honestly I think  they simply exist to make the main plan  seem more attractive for example you can  either get 20 plus apps for 55 per month  or you can just get Photoshop or just  Premiere Pro for 21 one dollars per  month at those prices many of you are  probably thinking that it would just be  better to Pirate the software and for  hobbyists and Freelancers that might  make sense but for Pure professionals  and small companies that simply isn't an  option these people are in a weird  Middle Ground where they feel that the  software is overpriced but at the same  time their business is making too much  money for them to be dealing with the  Glitchy pirated software also if they  have significant skill they don't want  to risk getting sued by Adobe so they  have no choice but to reluctantly pay  the price and judging by adobe's  financials this is what most  professionals ended up doing in the last  8 years adobe's annual earnings have  skyrocketed from 200 to 300 million  dollars to roughly 5 billion dollars  mostly thanks to the new subscription  model but while this move worked out  great financially this move put off most  of adobe's customers many of them  started looking for Alternatives but the  reality is that there are no  alternatives for most Adobe apps sure  you've got options like DaVinci Resolve  Sony Vegas anacard Pro and a blender for  video editing but if you're looking for  things like Lightroom and illustrator  animate or photoshop you have no choice  but to go with Adobe I mean there's a  reason they control that 90 of the  market and now Adobe had turned on the  milking switch  now that adobe is making all this extra  money serving the same Market you would  think that they would amp up the  customer experience this means less bugs  fewer crashes better customer support  and a greater compatibility but the  company has actually been trending it in  the exact opposite direction ever since  they launched the subscription model  mostly because they just don't have to  care before the subscription model it  was essential that adobe made each  iteration as significant and impactful  as possible this was the only way that  they were gonna convince new users to  get a license or existing users to  upgrade their license with the  subscription model however Adobe has a  30 million users logged in depending it  every single month or every single year  regardless of whether the software  improves or not at the very least you  would hope that they would maintain a  certain bar but even that's not the case  with every new iteration of Windows Mac  OS and G views it seems like Adobe  software simply gets buggier and a  glitchier because they're not keeping up  with the changes and if you try to  contact them about these issues you're  probably going to be ignored it just  like all these people aside from letting  their software standards slip it seems  like Adobe has also let their ethical  standards slip for years Adobe was able  to just sit back and watch their income  figures go up as more and more people  reluctantly switched over but this  upward Trend it came to a harsh ended  during the pandemic basically everyone  who is on the fence about Adobe  capitulated and subscribed during the  pandemic in other words Adobe reached  Market saturation with their  subscriptions and their net income has  actually been flat for two years now  this isn't exactly a Bad Thing given  that they're making over a billion  dollars at every single quarter but as  you might have guessed they're looking  for more growth the easiest way to  accomplish this is to just in-tube  subscription prices and you can bet that  they've been doing that but they've also  turned to more unethical means in recent  times for example judging by reviews  there seems to be a trend of adobe  locking in free trial users into annual  subscriptions and when you try to cancel  your hit with a 100 to 150 termination  fee something else that's put off many  users is adobe's device limitations even  if you have a license you're only  allowed to authenticate two devices at  any given time but if you're a creative  professional you often use more than two  devices on a regular basis for example  you might have a main PC a Macbook a  Windows laptop and maybe an iPad but if  you want to use all these devices you'll  be regularly signing in and out and  authenticating and the authenticating  various devices this might sound like a  first world problem but let's not forget  that people are paying 600 every single  year for this software and they can't  even use it on all of their devices the  only reason that Adobe doesn't offer a  more generous device limit like 5 is to  get more people to sign up for their  commercial licenses which cost 85 per  month for each license so not only does  Adobe milk their customers but they  often do it in unethical ways  in stark contrast all of this Adobe is a  champion amongst employees and the  secret behind this is pretty simple they  treat employees at how they want to be  treated starting with offering an  extraordinary flexibility even before  the pandemic Adobe had strong remote and  hybrid work programs they also don't  micromanage any of their employees in  fact they don't even check if employees  are at work during traditional working  hours of course you might have to be  present at certain meetings at certain  times but other than that you're free to  create your own working schedule and  you'll be judged solely on results if  you're able to work it just 10 hours a  week and produce results that they're  happy with good for you aside from  giving employees Freedom Adobe also  makes employees feel valued by giving  them significant responsibility for  example they offer unlimited paid time  off because they trust that employees  have the best gauge of when and how much  time they need to take off they also  give employees significant Freedom  regarding what they work on but likely  the best example of adobe's  responsibility-driven culture is their  award-winning kickbox program this  program gives any staff member who  requests said a one thousand dollar  prepaid credit card to explore their  ideas no questions asked 1 000 employees  have used this program and that 23 of  these ideas ended up receiving it  further investment Adobe only has a  total of 25 000 employees meaning that 1  in 25 took this initiative and tried to  create larger impact all of this that  leads us to adobe's Core Company culture  value which is integrating up a  people-centric approach oftentimes one  of the top complaints at many companies  is that they don't recognize or  appreciate their employees but Adobe has  a robust Awards program that seeks to  recognize their best employees every  single year the most prestigious of  these Awards is the founders award which  is given to 12 people annually adopia  also takes is a great care when it comes  to encouraging diversity ensuring  paperity and creating transparency with  all these perks it's no wonder why Adobe  is so cherished by their employees  as we can see creating a great workplace  culture isn't rocket science it just  comes down to offering flexibility  Freedom responsibility and recognition  but ironically these are the exact  sectors in which Adobe is lacking when  it comes to their business  they don't offer customers any  flexibility or Freedom basically  everyone is forced to get the Creative  Cloud subscription similarly Adobe is  taking no responsibility when it comes  to maintaining refining and upgrading  the user experience and finally they're  not really recognizing or acknowledging  their customers they offer crappy  customer support and several legitimate  concerns are just outright ignored most  companies wouldn't not be able to get  away with such Behavior but given  adobe's Monopoly within creative  software they're able to do just about  anything and while there is a vocal  minority that tries to spread awareness  and is ditching Adobe for things like  Affinity photo the vast majority  unfortunately just don't care enough and  this includes myself as well 